## Context

Author directive, 2026-10-27: management by intention, under KISS, less is
more, and Trust, Verify, Monitor. The orchestrator says what to verify and
why; gaze owns the how. This replaces the brief grammar of the 2026-10-26
version of this ticket (PR 1686, closed unmerged: too much ceremony, a
170-line resolver and a seat-manifest check policing the caller's wording).

Why the earlier attempts did not take. Attempt 1 (tracker [[1100]]): the
caller wrote the review plan, under a risk floor. Attempt 2 (ticket 1176):
gaze chose with no input and defaulted to its full panel. Attempt 3 (PR
1686): the caller wrote seats and scope in a grammar. Here the caller writes
only intent; the what bounds the scope, the why sets the risk, gaze plans.

Evidence (2026-10-26 night): PR 1677, about 200 lines, took four full panels;
PR 1676 took thirteen gaze rounds, most re-reading 1,200 lines to check a fix
of a few lines. Ticket 1197's measurement (docs/2026-10-27-gaze-wall-time.md):
setup and closing turns are 42 percent of gaze wall time; a narrower panel
saves at most 32 s per run, so the gain sought here is fewer and smaller
rounds, not a faster round.

## Design

1. **Intent in.** `/gaze <pr> verify <what> because <why>`, plain words. No
   intent: the what is the whole merge request, the why its ticket's exit
   criteria. Nothing is refused for its wording.
2. **Plan.** Gaze writes a short plan before launching anything: method,
   workers, budget. It derives them from the change's nature (prose or code),
   size, location (the risk rows of skills/route/references/decorrelation.md)
   and history (what earlier results on this merge request covered). The
   cheapest method that settles the what wins: running a test against old and
   new code, one seat, a panel, an external or other-family model, a weaker
   or stronger model, chosen through the route skill.
3. **Execute.** Exactly the plan, nothing added.
4. **Verify.** Plan against what was done: each planned item ran and
   reported; each worker is the model or family planned; the run covered the
   stated what; the budget held. A deviation is named with its reason. A
   planned perspective missing without a substitute that still settles the
   what means the verdict cannot be REVIEWED.
5. **Record.** One result comment: intent, plan, what ran (worker, model,
   time), plan check, findings, verdict (REVIEWED, FINDINGS or ESCALATE;
   gaze never approves), duration. That comment is the whole register.

Properties of every run:

- **One pass.** Gaze reviews once and returns. No internal round, no fix
  loop: findings go back to the caller, who fixes and, if it wants, sends a
  new narrower intent.
- **Read-only on the code.** Gaze never edits, commits or pushes on the
  merge request's branch, nor writes in the caller's worktree.
- **Checks allowed, scratch clean.** Gaze may run tests or scripts to settle
  the what, in a throwaway worktree of its own; it removes that worktree and
  every scratch file before returning, and the record says so.
- **One delivery place.** The report is the merge request's gaze result
  comment, the same text returned to the caller; nothing else is written.

Trust: the orchestrator trusts gaze's plan; gaze trusts the coder's tests and
the gate's checks. Verify: step 4, and the comment shows intent beside plan.
Monitor: trace-doctor and the 1197 telemetry judge plan sizing across runs;
guidance changes on that evidence, never through a per-run floor.

A later round is a new, narrower intent ("verify the fixes for findings X
and Y"), not a re-run of the whole review. Memory files every session reads
(ticket 1223, folded in earlier) are covered the same way: an orchestrator
that wants them reviewed says so in the intent.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `/gaze` takes an optional plain-text intent (what, why) after the merge request number; with none, the default intent is the whole merge request against its ticket's exit criteria.
- [ ] Gaze writes its plan (method, workers, budget) before launching anything, from nature, size, location and history; the guidance for it lives in one place and states the "cheapest method that settles the what" rule with a few worked cases.
- [ ] After executing, gaze compares plan with what was done and records the outcome as a `plan check:` line; a planned perspective missing without a valid substitute blocks a REVIEWED verdict.
- [ ] The result comment carries intent, plan, run, plan check, findings, verdict and duration, and is the only register (no per-seat attribution writes into the merge request body).
- [ ] One pass: gaze has no internal round or fix loop; it returns after its single review.
- [ ] Read-only: a test shows a gaze run leaves the merge request's branch tip and the caller's worktree unchanged.
- [ ] Scratch clean: checks run in gaze's own throwaway worktree, removed with every scratch file before it returns; a test shows nothing of the run remains on disk.
- [ ] `/hunt`, `/raid` and `/merge` pass an intent and drop their fixed round loops: a later round is a new, narrower intent.
- [ ] The fixed battery as a default, the brief grammar idea, and per-phase integrity rules the plan check replaces are deleted; the skill text is shorter than before.
- [ ] Regression tests: a small intent yields a one-worker plan; a plan item that did not run makes the plan check fail and blocks REVIEWED.
- [ ] The harness gate (`.merge-gate.json` checks) passes.
